<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ras</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ras, meaning head, was one of the highest noble and military titles in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a>, below that of the negus. It was held by powerful regional lords and governors. Holders of the title commanded armies and administered provinces, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a> was known as Ras Tafari before his coronation.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Zemene Mesafint, the Era of the Princes beginning in 1769, represented the complete triumph of regional power: a long series of puppet emperors occupied the throne for eighty-six years while real authority passed to <b>Ras</b> regents of the Yejju Oromo dynasty and rival provincial warlords.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>They were excavated in the 1950s from the Royal Palace at Ugarit, the present-day <b>Ras</b> Shamra on the coast of northern Syria, in a stratum dating from the fourteenth century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hurrian-songs">Hurrian Hymn No. 6 (Hymn to Nikkal)</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ras</b> Tafari Makonnen was crowned Emperor Haile Selassie I in Addis Ababa before foreign dignitaries.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-2">2 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Ethiopian forces under <b>Ras</b> Alula defeated the Mahdist Sudanese of Osman Digna at Kufit after two days of fighting.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-23">23 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>His successors of the Vukanović line ruled from <b>Ras</b>, often as Byzantine or Hungarian clients.</blockquote><a href="/state/grand-principality-of-serbia">Grand Principality of Serbia</a></li><li><blockquote>Prince Časlav restored it with Byzantine help from the late 920s, but after his death in the mid-10th century the state fragmented and Byzantium took <b>Ras</b> around 970.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-serbia-early-medieval">Principality of Serbia (early medieval)</a></li></ul></section>
